Whilst the modern [[Audi]] company has never entered [[Grand Prix motor racing]], its predecessor company [[Auto Union]] had [[Auto Union racing cars|competed in Grand Prix racing]] from [[1935 Grand Prix season|1935]] to [[1939 Grand Prix season|1939]], prior to [[World War II]] and the inception of the [[Formula One World Championship]] in {{F1|1950}}.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hughes |first1=Mark |title=Audi has gone grand prix racing before - sort of |url=https://www.the-race.com/formula-1/audi-has-gone-grand-prix-racing-before-sort-of/ |access-date=22 April 2025 |work=The Race |date=26 August 2022 |language=en}}</ref> 

Auto Union was founded in 1932, during the [[Great Depression]], as a merger of four struggling automotive firms: Audi, [[DKW]], [[Horch]], and [[Wanderer (car)|Wanderer]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Porsche Designs: From Racetrack to Battlefield|date=27 January 2012|url=http://ranwhenparked.net/2012/01/27/porsche-designs-from-racetrack-to-battlefield/|publisher=Ran When Parked|access-date=3 February 2012|archive-date=5 February 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120205150550/http://ranwhenparked.net/2012/01/27/porsche-designs-from-racetrack-to-battlefield/|url-status=live}}</ref> The following year, German Chancellor [[Adolf Hitler]] announced a state-sponsored motor racing programme with [[Mercedes-Benz]].<ref name="DD2009">{{cite web|url=http://www.ddavid.com/formula1/auto_c.htm|title=Auto Union Type C|publisher=DDavid.com|access-date=2009-06-20|archive-date=10 October 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121010180201/http://www.ddavid.com/formula1/auto_c.htm|url-status=dead}}</ref> Upon request from [[Ferdinand Porsche]], Hitler agreed that competition between two German firms would improve their chances of victory and [[Nationalism and sport|national glory]]; an annual {{currency|500,000|RM}} ({{currency|40,000|GBP}}; £{{formatnum:{{inflation|UK|40,000|1933|r=-4}}}} in {{Inflation/year|UK}}) prize for the most successful car between Auto Union and Mercedes-Benz was announced, eventually rising to 3,125,000 ℛℳ (£250,000; £{{formatnum:{{inflation|UK|250,000|1933|r=-4}}}} in {{Inflation/year|UK}}) and prompting [[Silver Arrows|a heated rivalry]].<ref>Setright, L. J. K. "Mercedes-Benz: The German Fountain-head", in Northey, Tom, ed. ''World of Automobiles'' (London: Orbis, 1974), Vol. 11, p. 1311–1312.</ref>

Across five seasons in the [[AIACR European Championship]], Auto Union won seven ''[[Grand Prix motor racing#First Grand Prix and the Grandes Épreuves|Grandes Épreuves]]'' and 14 [[Glossary of motorsport terms#N|non-championship]] Grands Prix.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.goldenera.fi/main.htm|title=THE GOLDEN ERA – OF GRAND PRIX RACING|work=kolumbus.fi|access-date=24 January 2025|archive-date=6 June 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110606091347/http://www.kolumbus.fi/leif.snellman/main.htm|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Bernd Rosemeyer]] claimed European Drivers' Championship in [[1936 Grand Prix season|1936]], driving the [[Auto Union Type C|Type C]], with victories at the [[1936 German Grand Prix|German]], [[1936 Swiss Grand Prix|Swiss]], and [[1936 Italian Grand Prix|Italian]] Grands Prix.<ref name="DD2009"/> [[Hermann Paul Müller]]—driving the [[Auto Union Type D|Type D]]—unofficially won the final edition in [[1939 Grand Prix season|1939]], with the title not awarded due to the onset of [[World War II in Europe]] following the [[invasion of Poland]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Unfinished Symphony: Why the 1939 European Championship was never won |work=8W |url=http://8w.forix.com/ec1939.html |first=Richard |last=Armstrong |date=November 7, 2002 |accessdate = 2007-08-05 }}</ref> Auto Union was later absorbed by [[Volkswagen]] in 1964, becoming the modern-day Audi company.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://gearheads.org/the-history-of-audi-auto-group/|title=The History of Audi Auto Group|last=Nazario|first=Uriah|website=GearHeads|access-date=20 March 2015|date=18 May 2012|archive-date=19 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150319071651/http://gearheads.org/the-history-of-audi-auto-group/ }}</ref>

=== Zürich-based constructors (1993–2025) ===
{{Main article|Sauber|BMW Sauber|Alfa Romeo Racing}}
{{See also|Sauber Grand Prix results|Alfa Romeo Grand Prix results}}

[[File:Robert_Kubica_2008_Canada_2.jpg|thumb|right|[[Sauber]] were previously partnered with [[Mercedes-Benz in Formula One|Mercedes-Benz]], [[Ford Motor Company|Ford]], [[Scuderia Ferrari|Ferrari]] (including [[Sauber Petronas Engineering|Petronas]] rebadging era), [[BMW in Formula One|BMW]], and [[Alfa Romeo in Formula One|Alfa Romeo]]; it achieved its only victory at the [[2008 Canadian Grand Prix]] with BMW and [[Robert Kubica]].|alt=Robert Kubica driving the BMW Sauber F1.08 at the 2008 Canadian Grand Prix]]
The antecedent constructor to Audi based in [[Hinwil]]—[[Sauber]]—made its Formula One debut in {{F1|1993}}, having contested [[sportscar racing]] since 1970.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Shah |first=Kunal |date=2020-06-29 |title=Peter Sauber Interview On 50 Years In Motorsport |url=https://www.kunalsf1blog.com/peter-sauber-50-years-motorsport/ |access-date=2025-07-13 |website=Kunal's F1 Blog |language=en-US}}</ref> The team was partnered with [[Mercedes-Benz in Formula One|Mercedes-Benz]] until {{F1|1994}}, [[Ford Motor Company|Ford]] until {{F1|1996}}, and [[Scuderia Ferrari|Ferrari]] (rebadged as [[Sauber Petronas Engineering|Petronas]]) until {{F1|2005}}, before becoming the [[factory team]] for [[BMW in Formula One|BMW]] from {{F1|2006}} to {{F1|2009}}.<ref>{{Cite magazine |url=https://www.motorsportmagazine.com/database/teams/sauber |url-access=registration |title=Sauber {{!}} Motorsport Database |magazine=[[Motor Sport (magazine)|Motor Sport]] |access-date=8 November 2025 |issn=0027-2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251108163958/https://www.motorsportmagazine.com/database/teams/sauber/ |archive-date=8 November 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Sauber claimed its highest finishes in the [[World Constructors' Championship]] with second and third in {{F1|2007}} and {{F1|2008}}, respectively, the former after [[2007 Formula One espionage controversy|a disqualification]] for [[McLaren]] and the latter including its sole victory at the {{F1GP|2008|Canadian}} with [[Robert Kubica]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.statsf1.com/en/bmw-sauber/grand-prix.aspx |title=BMW Sauber – Grands Prix started |publisher=StatsF1 |accessdate=16 April 2018}}</ref> It returned as an independent constructor in {{F1|2010}} with [[Scuderia Ferrari|Ferrari]] engines, repurchased by [[Peter Sauber]] following the [[Great Recession]] and [[Qadbak Investments|a failed shell company acquisition]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/80350|title=BMW sells F1 team back to Peter Sauber|publisher=[[Haymarket Group|Haymarket Publications]]|work=autosport.com|author=Pablo Elizalde|date=27 November 2009|access-date=28 November 2009|archive-date=30 November 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091130021829/http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/80350|url-status=live}}</ref> A title sponsorship deal with [[Alfa Romeo in Formula One|Alfa Romeo]] in {{F1|2018}} led to a full rebrand from {{F1|2019}} to {{F1|2023}},<ref>{{cite news |title=Formula 1: Sauber renamed Alfa Romeo Racing from start of 2019 season |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/47089294 |access-date=2 February 2019 |work=bbc.co.uk |agency=BBC News |publisher=BBC Sport |date=1 February 2019 |archive-date=22 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230422214806/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/formula1/47089294 |url-status=live }}</ref> after which it switched to support from [[Stake (online casino)|Stake]] and [[Kick (service)|Kick]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Cooper |first=Adam |date=15 December 2023 |title=Sauber to run under Stake F1 Team name in 2024-25 |url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/sauber-stake-f1-team-name-2024/10558037/?fbclid=IwAR3rtkd_PXzlAhzkwOaC8HdsakqHWriT9aC2Cc-UCArWaTHh0YtUe4vUilc |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231215091855/https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/sauber-stake-f1-team-name-2024/10558037/ |archive-date=15 December 2023 |access-date=15 December 2023 |website=Motorsport.com |language=en}}</ref>

== Constructor history ==
=== Establishment (2022–2025) ===
In August 2022, Audi announced that it would enter Formula One as a [[Formula One engines|power unit]] manufacturer in {{F1|2026}}—the planned year for a regulation overhaul.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Audi to join Formula 1 from 2026 |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-audi-to-join-formula-1-from-2026.yr9pFVd5nSQBbZ7EZZDLJ.html |access-date=26 August 2022 |website=www.formula1.com |language=en |archive-date=26 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220826072224/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-audi-to-join-formula-1-from-2026.yr9pFVd5nSQBbZ7EZZDLJ.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Kisby |first=Cambridge |date=2024-03-14 |title=Audi's Sauber takeover: Everything you need to know before 2026 F1 entry |url=https://www.motorsportmagazine.com/articles/single-seaters/f1/audis-sauber-takeover-everything-you-need-to-know-before-2026-f1-entry/ |access-date=2024-05-10 |website=[[Motor Sport (magazine)|Motor Sport]]|language=en-GB |archive-date=10 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240510044657/https://www.motorsportmagazine.com/articles/single-seaters/f1/audis-sauber-takeover-everything-you-need-to-know-before-2026-f1-entry/ |url-status=live }}</ref> That October, Audi confirmed its rumoured partnership with [[Sauber]], acquiring a stake in the company for a full rebrand and power unit deal.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Audi selects Sauber as strategic partner for Formula 1 entry |url=https://www.audi-mediacenter.com/en/press-releases/audi-selects-sauber-as-strategic-partner-for-formula-1-entry-14981 |access-date=27 October 2022 |website=Audi MediaCenter |language=en |archive-date=27 October 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221027074923/https://www.audi-mediacenter.com/en/press-releases/audi-selects-sauber-as-strategic-partner-for-formula-1-entry-14981 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Sauber to become Audi works F1 team from 2026 {{!}} Formula 1® |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-sauber-to-become-audi-works-f1-team-from-2026.6EMJQ2LlvshgpCXdCR9xJl.html |access-date=27 October 2022 |website=www.formula1.com |language=en |archive-date=5 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230105022002/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-sauber-to-become-audi-works-f1-team-from-2026.6EMJQ2LlvshgpCXdCR9xJl.html |url-status=live }}</ref> In November 2024, the [[Qatar Investment Authority]] purchased a minority stake in the team.<ref>{{cite web |title=Qatar Investmemt Authority Buys Stake In Audi F1 Racing Team |url=https://gulfnews.com/world/gulf/qatar/qatar-investment-authority-buys-stake-in-audi-f1-racing-team-1.1732905924350 |website=[[Gulf News]] |date=23 November 2024}}</ref> The investment was described as a "substantial minority" stake in Sauber Holding AG, with Audi and QIA stating that the funding would support the expansion of personnel and facilities ahead of the team's planned 2026 entry.<ref>{{Cite web |title=QIA acquire minority share in future Audi team |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/qatar-investment-authority-acquire-substantial-minority-share-in-future-audi.5L8Q5liKiGYQ25XqP8BE7C |access-date=2026-02-18 |website=www.formula1.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Audi F1 Project strengthened by investment from QIA |url=https://www.audi-mediacenter.com/en/press-releases/audi-f1-project-strengthened-by-investment-from-qia-16415 |access-date=2026-02-18 |website=Audi MediaCenter |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=November 29, 2024 |title=Qatar adds Audi Formula One team to its sporting line-up |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/qia-takes-stake-audi-f1-team-2024-11-29/}}</ref> 

Veteran driver [[Nico Hülkenberg]] and rookie [[Gabriel Bortoleto]] joined Sauber on separate multi-year contracts from {{F1|2025}} onwards, thus becoming Audi's driver lineup upon its debut.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Nico Hulkenberg confirmed as first Audi F1 driver after move from Haas to Sauber for 2025 confirmed |url=https://www.skysports.com/f1/news/12433/13123183/nico-hulkenberg-confirmed-as-first-audi-f1-driver-after-move-from-haas-to-sauber-for-2025-confirmed |access-date=26 April 2024 |website=Sky Sports |language=en |archive-date=26 April 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240426090051/https://www.skysports.com/f1/news/12433/13123183/nico-hulkenberg-confirmed-as-first-audi-f1-driver-after-move-from-haas-to-sauber-for-2025-confirmed |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Walsh |first=Fergal |date=2024-05-09 |title=Hulkenberg reveals 'driving factor' behind Audi F1 decision |url=https://racingnews365.com/hulkenberg-reveals-driving-factor-behind-audi-f1-decision |access-date=2024-05-10 |website=RacingNews365 |language=en |archive-date=7 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240807072540/https://racingnews365.com/hulkenberg-reveals-driving-factor-behind-audi-f1-decision |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Barretto|first=Lawrence|title=Kick Sauber confirm rookie Bortoleto as second driver for 2025|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/breaking-kick-sauber-confirm-rookie-bortoleto-as-second-driver-for-2025.2EL5ciR1wh3baHcobZqGl9 |date=6 November 2024 |publisher=formula1.com|language=en}}</ref> [[Neel Jani]] was signed as a [[sim racing|simulator]] driver to assist with car development.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Audi announce new simulator driver as they take next step in F1 power unit development|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/audi-announce-new-simulator-driver-as-they-take-next-step-in-f1-power-unit.4OMqlgC3THNtQRVoEa65kL|access-date=6 May 2024|website=Formula 1|archive-date=6 May 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240506062252/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/audi-announce-new-simulator-driver-as-they-take-next-step-in-f1-power-unit.4OMqlgC3THNtQRVoEa65kL|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-06-22 |last=Cozens |first=Jack |title=Audi brings in Porsche veteran as 2026 F1 engine sim driver |url=https://www.the-race.com/formula-1/audi-brings-in-porsche-veteran-as-2026-f1-engine-sim-driver/ |access-date=2024-05-10 |website=The Race |language=en |archive-date=10 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240510044658/https://www.the-race.com/formula-1/audi-brings-in-porsche-veteran-as-2026-f1-engine-sim-driver/ |url-status=live }}</ref> By July of that year, Sauber opened the ''Sauber Motorsport Technology Centre'' in [[Bicester Motion]], Oxfordshire,<ref>{{Cite web |title=Sauber Motorsport Opens Technology Centre at Bicester Motion, UK |url=https://www.sauber-group.com/corporate/news/detail/sauber-motorsport-opens-technology-centre-at-bicester-motion-uk|access-date=1 July 2025|language=en}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=Sauber open new UK Technology Centre as transformation into Audi continues |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/sauber-open-new-uk-technology-centre-as-transformation-into-audi-continues.2rXNDWnkLmC1P3KWM8TscJ|access-date=1 July 2025|language=en}}</ref> with the aim of attracting specialised personnel to join the team in the "[[Glossary of motorsport terms#M|Motorsport Valley]]" region of England. The technology centre aims to complement the primary headquarters in [[Hinwil]], Switzerland, where the chassis is constructed.<ref name=":0" /> Audi signed a multi-year deal with British [[financial technology]] company [[Revolut]] to become the title sponsor of the team.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Baldwin |first=Alan |date=2025-07-30 |title=Revolut to become title partner of Audi F1 team |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/finance/revolut-become-title-partner-audi-f1-team-2025-07-30/ |access-date=2025-07-30 |work=Reuters |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Audi's F1 team explained: the rocky road to 2026 launch |url=https://www.motorsportmagazine.com/articles/single-seaters/f1/audis-sauber-takeover-everything-you-need-to-know-before-2026-f1-entry |date=23 July 2024 |last=Kisby |first=Cambridge |access-date=3 November 2024 |website=[[Motor Sport (magazine)|Motor Sport]]}}</ref> In December 2025, the team unveiled its official Audi Revolut F1 Team name and logo, while Sauber Motorsport AG was set to be renamed Audi Motorsport AG as part of the transition.<ref name=":1" />  

=== Debut (2026) ===
[[File:2026 Chinese GP - Audi - Gabriel Bortoleto - Qualifying.jpg|thumb|Gabriel Bortoleto's [[Audi R26]] qualifying for the [[2026 Chinese Grand Prix]]]]
At the team's Berlin launch in January 2026, Audi said it had begun a five-year plan aimed at challenging for championships by 2030.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Amann |first=Christina |date=January 20, 2026 |title=Audi F1 team launch debut livery as title countdown starts |url=https://www.reuters.com/sports/formula1/audi-f1-team-launch-debut-livery-title-countdown-starts-2026-01-20/}}</ref> Audi debuted at the season-opening {{F1GP|2026|Australian}} in {{F1|2026}} with the [[Audi R26|R26]], designed for [[2026 Formula One World Championship#Regulation changes|new chassis and power unit regulations]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mitchell-Malm |first=Scott |date=12 November 2025 |title=Audi reveals new concept livery for its F1 debut in 2026 |url=https://www.the-race.com/formula-1/audi-reveals-new-concept-livery-for-its-f1-debut-in-2026/ |access-date=12 November 2025 |website=The Race |language=en}}</ref> [[Gabriel Bortoleto]] and [[Nico Hülkenberg]] qualified 10th and 11th respectively. Bortoleto finished the race in 9th place, scoring Audi's first ever points in Formula One, while Hülkenberg was unable to take the start due to technical issues. Roles were reversed in [[2026 Chinese Grand Prix|China]], with Bortoleto unable to start as Hülkenberg brought home a P11, after unfortunate [[safety car]] timing. The unluckiness of the safety car continued in [[2026 Japanese Grand Prix|Japan]], where Hülkenberg brought home another P11, while Bortoleto settled for 13th after a poor start.

The team would not finish in the points again until the [[2026 British Grand Prix|British Grand Prix]], where Bortoleto finished 8th under the safety car. More points would follow for Bortoleto in the [[2026 Belgian Grand Prix|Belgium]], where another P8 finish would score the team their first consecutive points. Hülkenburg would score his first points of the season, finishing in P9 in another consecutive points finish at the [[2026 Hungarian Grand Prix|Hungarian Grand Prix]]. This allowed Audi to overtake [[Williams Racing|Williams]] for 8th place in the Constructors' standings by one point.

== Team information ==

=== Constructor personnel ===
From its establishment, [[Jonathan Wheatley]] was the inaugural team principal of Audi.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |date=15 December 2025 |title=Audi Revolut F1 Team reveals official name and Berlin launch date |url=https://www.audi-mediacenter.com/en/press-releases/audi-revolut-f1-team-reveals-official-name-and-berlin-launch-date-16942 |access-date=23 December 2025 |website=Audi MediaCenter |language=en}}</ref> Following his departure in March 2026, project head [[Mattia Binotto]] was appointed Team Principal.<ref>{{Cite web |date=20 March 2026 |title=Audi announce team boss Wheatley to leave with immediate effect |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/audi-announce-team-boss-wheatley-to-leave-with-immediate-effect.1WqcMA6UUNOuruqoQcPb3Q |access-date=20 March 2026 |website=F1 |language=en}}</ref> [[James Key (Formula One)|James Key]] serves as Audi's Technical Director.<ref>{{Cite web |last=NewsDesk |date=22 February 2025 |title=James Key: We believe we have made solid progress |url=https://www.grandprix247.com/2025/02/22/james-key-we-believe-we-have-made-solid-progress/ |access-date=23 December 2025 |website=GrandPrix247 |language=en-US}}</ref> 

On 24 April 2026, it was announced that [[Allan McNish]], the current Director of the [[Audi Driver Development Programme|Driver Development Programme]], would assume the role of Racing Director, being accountable for trackside operations at a Grand Prix weekend. In addition, [[Mattia Binotto]]'s title was changed to CEO and Team Principal of the team.<ref>{{Cite web |date=24 April 2026 |title=Audi Revolut F1 Team appoints Allan McNish as Racing Director |url=https://www.audif1.com/en/app/news/2026/Allan-McNish-RacingDirector |access-date=24 April 2026 |website=audif1.com |language=en}}</ref>

=== Driver development programme ===
{{See also|Audi Driver Development Programme}}

On 23 January 2026, Audi launched their [[driver development programme]], being led by former Formula One driver [[Allan McNish]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Audi announce Driver Development Programme with Allan McNish as Director|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/audi-announce-driver-development-programme-with-allan-mcnish-as-director.Ox14nYFNhgiHkx9o6GGCc|website=Formula1.com|access-date=23 January 2026|language=en|date=23 January 2026}}</ref>

=== Power unit programme ===
The Audi [[Formula One engines|power unit]] programme will be operated by the subsidiary Audi Formula Racing GmbH,<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-03-11 |title=Audi accelerates F1 acquisition |url=https://magazine.audi.com.au/article/audi-accelerates-f1-acquisition |access-date=2024-06-09 |website=Audi Magazine Australia |publisher=[[Audi]] |language=en |archive-date=9 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240609052945/https://magazine.audi.com.au/article/audi-accelerates-f1-acquisition |url-status=live }}</ref> based in [[Neuburg an der Donau]], Bavaria, Germany.<ref name="engine">{{Cite web |title=Audi expands Competence Center Motorsport for its Formula 1 project |url=https://www.audi-mediacenter.com/en/press-releases/audi-expands-competence-center-motorsport-for-its-formula-1-project-15068 |access-date=6 May 2024 |website=Audi MediaCenter |language=en |archive-date=7 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240507030653/https://www.audi-mediacenter.com/en/press-releases/audi-expands-competence-center-motorsport-for-its-formula-1-project-15068 |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Racing licence and facilities ===
Audi competes with a [[List of Formula One constructors#Team's nationality|German racing licence]]<ref>{{cite web|title=Audi|url=https://www.statsf1.com/en/audi.aspx|publisher=StatsF1|access-date=14 January 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=van Denderen |first1=Ludo |title=Bad news for Swiss F1 fans: Audi opts for German national anthem |url=https://www.gpblog.com/en/news/audi-lets-sauber-race-under-german-flag.html |access-date=1 May 2026 |work=GPBlog |date=2 April 2024 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250617061201/https://www.gpblog.com/en/news/audi-lets-sauber-race-under-german-flag.html |archive-date=17 June 2025}}</ref> and is based at three facilities across Europe:
* Chassis department: former [[Sauber]] headquarters in [[Hinwil]], Zurich, Switzerland;
* Technology centre: [[Bicester Motion]] in [[Bicester]], Oxfordshire, England;<ref>{{Cite web |title=Sauber open new UK Technology Centre as transformation into Audi continues |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/sauber-open-new-uk-technology-centre-as-transformation-into-audi-continues.2rXNDWnkLmC1P3KWM8TscJ|access-date=1 July 2025|language=en}}</ref>
* Power unit department: [[Neuburg an der Donau]], Bavaria, Germany.<ref name="engine"/>

